Changes from upstream (spec P4 §4.2): the related-ticket block, the
omitted-ticket notice, the question/answer block and the duplicated example
(duplicate_prompt_examples) are removed. Kept verbatim: the --PR Info--
block (date, title, branch, description fenced with ======), the diff
fenced with ====== and the final line followed by an open ```yaml fence
(DQ-7; the YAML loader's sign-off handling depends on it). The pipeline's
one re-ask inserts its note on its own line before the final line.
Jinja whitespace control is reproduced with {{- and -}}.

Intentional deviation (spec P7 §5.2, X-13): upstream has no existing-
discussion block. When the pipeline supplies one (.discussion, already
rendered: the fixed header and the threads inside an adaptive code fence),
it follows the description and precedes the diff; without one nothing is
rendered, so the prompt is byte-identical to the one without the feature.

Intentional deviation (v1.1 spec WP-11e2, X-19): upstream reviews a pull
request in one call. When the pipeline reviews a large one in several parts,
each part's prompt gets one line (.part_header, the fixed sentence of
PartHeader) right before the diff, telling the model which part it sees.
With one part nothing is rendered, so the prompt is byte-identical to the
one without the feature.

Intentional deviation (v1.1 spec WP-11c, X-22, RC-8): upstream has no
repository context. When context.repo.enabled is set and the pipeline found
uses of the changed symbols outside the pull request, it supplies one block
(.repo_context, already rendered: the fixed sentence of repoctx.Header and
the entries "[path:line] uses symbol" with their snippets inside an
adaptive code fence). It follows the discussion block and precedes the part
line and the diff; without one nothing is rendered, so the prompt is
byte-identical to the one without the feature. In a review in several parts
an entry whose file is itself changed by the pull request, in a file another
part reviews (or that no part reviews), ends its first line with a mark in
parentheses: "(changed in this pull request; reviewed in part J)" or
"(changed in this pull request; not reviewed)". The uses are read from the
head commit, so they show the new version of that file. The block's fixed intro sentence says that such entries are reviewed in another part or not at all.

Intentional deviation (X-31): upstream has no CI results. When review.ci_status
is on and the head commit has checks, the pipeline supplies one block (.ci,
already rendered: the fixed header of CIHeader and the failing and pending
checks inside an adaptive code fence). It follows the discussion block and
precedes the repository context; without one nothing is rendered, so the
prompt is byte-identical to the one without the feature.

Intentional deviation (X-32): upstream has no project guidelines and no
file tree. When review.guidelines_paths names a file that exists at the
pull request's base revision, the pipeline supplies .guidelines (the fixed
header of projctx.GuidelinesHeader, with the path, and the file's text
inside an adaptive code fence, cut at a line boundary to its budget); when
review.file_tree is on, it supplies .file_tree (the fixed header of
projctx.TreeHeader and the listing of the directories the change touches,
at the base revision, inside an adaptive code fence). Both follow the CI
block and precede the repository context, guidelines first; without them
nothing is rendered, so the prompt is byte-identical to the one without the
feature.

Intentional deviation (X-33): upstream has no focused review. When a
focused run's focus is a free text, the pipeline supplies .focus_block (the
fixed header of FocusHeader and "Focus only on: <text>" inside an adaptive
code fence). It follows the repository context and precedes the part line
and the diff; a known focus value and no focus render nothing here, so the
prompt is byte-identical to the one without the feature (the known values'
sentences are in the system prompt).

Intentional deviation (X-42, v2.2 spec V-2 §2.2): upstream's related-ticket
block is removed above; issue compliance has its own. When the run has an
issue (the issue_context argument or an issue the pull request names on its
own host) and the block fits next to the diff, the pipeline supplies .issue
(already rendered: the fixed sentence of issuectx.Header and the issues
inside an adaptive code fence, budgeted by review.max_issue_tokens). It
follows the description and precedes the discussion; without one nothing
is rendered, so the prompt is byte-identical to the one without the feature.
An incremental run with an issue also gets .issue_verdict right after it
(the fixed sentence of EarlierVerdictHeader and the verdict the last
review's overview recorded, in an adaptive code fence), when there is one.

Intentional deviation (X-41, v2.3 spec V-13a §1.4): upstream has no related
pull requests. When the caller names related pull requests and their block
fits next to the diff, the pipeline supplies .related (the fixed sentence of
RelatedHeader and, per related pull request, its line and numbered diff,
contract files first, inside an adaptive code fence). It follows the issue
blocks and precedes the discussion; without one nothing is rendered.
